# ArduPlane defaults for the catapult-launched Delta-38.
#
# Loaded AFTER Tools/autotest/default_params/plane-jsbsim.parm (see
# Tools/autotest/pysim/vehicleinfo.py), so everything here overrides it.
# The base file is tuned for the Rascal trainer: AIRSPEED_CRUISE 22,
# AIRSPEED_MIN 10, ROLL_LIMIT_DEG 65 - all wrong for this airframe.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- airspeeds
# Measured from the FDM (standalone JSBSim, scripts/d38_cruise.xml):
#   stall            23.2 m/s at the typical 34.4 kg all-up
#                    (24.4 m/s at the 38 kg MTOW, = the 88 km/h in Q 3.1)
#   min power        ~30 m/s
#   level cruise     34 m/s at ~72% throttle, ~2.2 L/h  [matches Q 5.8a]
#   best climb       +4.9 m/s at 31 m/s, +4.7 m/s at 34 m/s, full throttle
#   idle glide       -5.5 m/s at 34 m/s (L/D ~5.4, windmilling prop)
#   max level speed  ~40 m/s / 145 km/h at full throttle
# Wing loading is 19.6 kg/m2 - much lighter than the Geran-2's 42 - so this
# airframe genuinely flies slower, and telling TECS to hold 45 m/s (the
# Geran-2 value) would peg it at full throttle for the whole mission.
AIRSPEED_MIN    27
AIRSPEED_CRUISE 34
AIRSPEED_MAX    44
# Cruise-ish trim throttle: measured 72% holds 34 m/s level. THR_MAX stays at
# 100 from the base file so the climb-out is not throttle limited.
TRIM_THROTTLE   72

# The catapult releases at ~35.7 m/s measured (SITL_CATAPULT_LBF=660, 0.4 s
# stroke), already well above stall, so rotation to the climb schedule should
# trigger the instant it leaves the rail. 26 is past the 23.2 m/s stall and
# comfortably below release.
TKOFF_ROTATE_SPD 26.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- TECS
# TECS's whole job is bookkeeping the aircraft's total energy, so both of these
# have to be the airframe's REAL numbers or every correction is scaled wrongly.
# Both measured in standalone JSBSim (scripts/d38_cruise.xml):
#   climb at THR_MAX and AIRSPEED_CRUISE (34 m/s):  +4.70 m/s
#   sink  at THR_MIN and AIRSPEED_CRUISE (34 m/s):  -5.5 m/s
# The stock SINK_MIN of 2.0 is the one that bites: this is a draggy
# low-aspect-ratio delta and its idle glide with a windmilling propeller is
# only L/D ~5.4, i.e. it sinks nearly 3x faster than the default claims. TECS
# therefore believed closing the throttle cost almost no energy and kept
# over-correcting - measured as a self-sustaining ~10 s limit cycle in cruise
# (+/-12 m of altitude, 32-36 m/s, throttle slamming between 50 and 100%).
# Giving TECS the true figures is what settles it; the damping terms below are
# then only a modest trim.
TECS_CLMB_MAX   4.5
TECS_SINK_MIN   5.5

TECS_TIME_CONST 6.0
TECS_THR_DAMP   0.7
TECS_PTCH_DAMP  0.4
TECS_INTEG_GAIN 0.20

# ------------------------------------------------------------ lateral limits
# AR is 1.61 and the span only 1.68 m, so full elevon deflection produces a
# very high roll rate (>300 deg/s) and the base file's 65 deg bank limit is an
# aerobatic manoeuvre for this airframe - the same over-banked spiral trap
# documented for the Geran-2. Cap the bank and lengthen L1 so heading capture
# does not slam into the limit.
ROLL_LIMIT_DEG  35
NAVL1_PERIOD    18

# --------------------------------------------------------------- throttle
# THE fix for "launches at the right speed, then sinks into the ground".
# Measured in SITL: with the default 100 %/s slew ArduPlane ramps the throttle
# from 0 to 100 % over ~1.3 s starting at arm — i.e. across the whole catapult
# stroke and the first second of flight. SIM_JSBSim.cpp only holds full
# throttle while ArduPilot's own command is below 5 %, so as soon as the ramp
# passes 5 % the real engine gets 20 %, then 44 %... The Geran-2 shrugs this off
# (it uses an <electric_engine>: power is PowerWatts*throttle, instantaneous).
# A real FGPiston cannot: rpm and manifold pressure collapse, and spinning the
# 0.0196 kg*m2 propeller back up to 6300 rpm on ~8 N*m takes ~1.6 s. Measured
# result was release at 33 m/s instead of 35.7, then no acceleration at all,
# a sustained lift deficit and ground contact 2.6 s later.
# A catapult launch is committed at full power on the rail, so there is no
# reason for a slew limit here at all.
TKOFF_THR_SLEW -1
THR_SLEWRATE    0

# ------------------------------------------------------------- pitch response
# Only the parameters that exist in ArduPlane 4.6: the rate loop is
# PTCH_RATE_*/RLL_RATE_* (the old PTCH2SRV_P/I/D names were removed in 4.1 and
# are silently ignored, which is a good way to think you have retuned something
# and have not).
#
# The base file's PTCH2SRV_TCONST of 0.25 is kept close: this airframe has a
# thin static margin (~9 % MAC) but a well-damped short period (Cmq -3.0 —
# verified, an elevator pulse settles in under 1 s with no overshoot), so it
# does not need a sluggish outer loop. A slower 0.75 was tried first and is the
# wrong direction: it demands a small pitch rate, the rate loop's feed-forward
# then asks for only ~17 % of elevon travel, and 17 % is not enough to rotate
# out of the post-launch mush (measured: nav_pitch 15 deg demanded, -6 deg
# achieved, servo2 stuck at 1584 of a 1500-2000 range).
PTCH2SRV_TCONST 0.35
PTCH2SRV_RMAX_UP 45
PTCH2SRV_RMAX_DN 45
RLL2SRV_TCONST  0.35
RLL2SRV_RMAX    60

# The base plane-jsbsim.parm sets SERVO2_REVERSED=1 for a different reference
# airframe. Systems/Aircraft control.xml already inverts the elevon pitch
# scale (an aft elevon needs TE-up for nose-up), so reversing the servo as
# well would reverse the pitch loop - the exact failure the Geran-2 file
# documents (elevator saturates fighting itself).
SERVO2_REVERSED 0

# Bit 2 (value 4) = DISABLE_TOFF_ATTITUDE_CHK. auto_takeoff_check()
# (ArduPlane/takeoff.cpp) otherwise requires pitch/roll inside bounds for its
# whole confirmation window before handing off to real TECS/attitude control,
# and until then the aircraft coasts on a fixed pre-launch surface output. A
# catapult provides a known-good launch the instant it fires, and the
# propeller's own torque reaction rolls this light airframe ~9 deg during the
# 0.4 s stroke (Ixx is only 1.85 kg*m2 against ~8 N*m of shaft torque), which
# is exactly the kind of transient that can make the attitude gate fail
# repeatedly and never confirm the launch.
FLIGHT_OPTIONS 4

ARMING_CHECK 0
